# .cursorrules
# Project: MoneyMentor AI — AI Financial Coach
# Stack: Next.js 14 + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS + Supabase + OpenAI GPT

Always use TypeScript strict mode and never use the any type; use unknown with type guards instead.
Keep React components under 150 lines; extract logic into custom hooks once a component grows beyond that.
Always wrap async OpenAI and Supabase calls in try/catch and return {error: string, data: null} on failure.
Never hardcode the OpenAI API key, Supabase URL/keys, or any database credentials; load all secrets via environment variables and keep .env.local out of version control.
Every API route must validate its input on the server side before processing, even if the client already validated it — reject invalid input before calling the OpenAI API.
Validate every structured AI response (e.g. the savings plan JSON) against its expected schema before returning it to the client; on failure, retry once, then fall back to a deterministic calculation.
Never let AI-generated copy recommend a specific brokerage, stock, or fund by name, and always show the educational-only disclaimer on a chat session's first message.
Enforce the disclaimer and no-specific-product rules at the API layer, not only in the UI, so a frontend change can't silently drop them.
Place API route handlers in /app/api, shared logic and prompt templates in /lib, and reusable UI in /components.
Every new API route must include at least one test covering a valid request and one covering an invalid-input case, matching the acceptance criteria in spec.md.
